  "live_records": {
    "q-278395": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278395",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14147016/assets/33adaa159f0926038c6b04a750b6a61b8a18e0fe2df9c6c3c581c139e27b2c95.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(6,0),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
