    "q-278400": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278400",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-1,$ so the center is located at $(2,-1).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=18$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=18,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
