    "q-278403": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278403",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{15}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{49}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=1$ and $k=6,$ so the center is located at $(1,6).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=15$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=15,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{15}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{15}=2\\sqrt{15}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
